<section style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#0A3D5C 0%,#1273A8 100%);padding:60px 24px 68px;">
  <div style="max-width:1220px;margin:0 auto;">
    <h1 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:clamp(30px,5vw,42px);font-weight:800;color:#fff;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 14px;letter-spacing:-0.02em;">Disclosure</h1>
    <p style="font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.85);max-width:620px;margin:0;">How this site works, who actually shows up to cut your foundation, and how we get paid. In plain terms.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:56px 24px 76px;">
  <div style="max-width:1220px;margin:0 auto;">
    <div class="legal">

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>The short version:</strong> Lincoln Egress Windows is a referral service, not an egress window contractor. We do not cut foundations, install windows, or dig window wells. When you call the number on this site you are connected to an independent contractor serving your area, and we may be paid for making that connection.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>What we do</h2>
      <p>We publish detailed information about egress windows in Lincoln and southeast Nebraska — what the work costs, what the IRC and Lincoln-Lancaster County require, how window well drainage actually works, and when a basement room legally counts as a bedroom. The point is that you reach a decision better informed than when you arrived.</p>
      <p>When you are ready to have the work done, we connect you by phone with an independent contractor serving your area.</p>

      <h2>What we do not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not cut foundation openings, install windows, or excavate window wells.</li>
        <li>We do not employ installers, concrete cutters, or excavation crews.</li>
        <li>We do not pull permits. The contractor you hire does that under their own license.</li>
        <li>We do not set the price you are quoted, and we are not party to your contract.</li>
        <li>We do not supervise, inspect, or guarantee the work performed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we are compensated</h2>
      <p>We are paid for connecting you with a contractor. That payment comes from the contractor or from an advertising network acting on their behalf, and it is not added to your bill. You pay the contractor directly, at whatever price the two of you agree.</p>
      <p>You should know this before you pick up the phone, which is why a short version appears in the footer of every page on this site rather than only here.</p>

      <h2>Licensing, insurance, and permits</h2>
      <p>Any statement on this site about licensing, insurance, or permit handling describes the standard we expect of the contractors we work with. It is not a warranty about the specific company that takes your job, because we are not that company.</p>
      <p><strong>Verify credentials yourself before work begins.</strong> This matters more than usual here, because cutting a foundation wall is structural work on the part of your house that holds it up. Specifically:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ask for a certificate of general liability insurance sent to you directly by the insurer, not a copy handed over by the contractor.</li>
        <li>Confirm workers' compensation coverage for everyone who will be on your property.</li>
        <li>Confirm who is pulling the permit and under whose license. A new foundation opening in Lincoln requires one, currently <strong>$75 to $200</strong>.</li>
        <li>For a concrete block foundation, ask specifically how the load above the opening will be carried. See <a href="/blog/block-vs-poured-foundation-egress/">block versus poured foundations</a>.</li>
        <li>Get the scope, the well, the drainage, and any interior restoration in writing before work starts.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Our <a href="/blog/hiring-egress-window-contractor-checklist/">contractor hiring checklist</a> covers this in more detail, and it applies equally to a contractor you reach through this site.</p>

      <h2>Pricing information on this site</h2>
      <p>Every price range published here is a general figure for the Lincoln area, gathered so you can budget and recognise a quote that is unusually high or suspiciously low. Ranges are never a quote, an offer, or a commitment. Only the contractor who inspects your foundation can price your job, and that number governs.</p>

      <h2>Availability and response times</h2>
      <p>Availability, hours, and how quickly someone can reach you depend on the contractor who receives your call and on conditions at the time. Same-day and 24/7 availability depend on provider participation, location, technician availability, and demand. We cannot guarantee a response time.</p>

      <h2>Safety</h2>
      <p>If someone is currently sleeping in a basement room with no compliant way out, treat that as the safety issue it is rather than a scheduling problem. A basement bedroom served only by an interior stairway has one exit, and a fire in the wrong place leaves none. Working smoke alarms on every level are the minimum in the meantime.</p>
      <p>If a window well is actively flooding into a finished basement, deal with the water and any electrical hazard first. Never enter standing water where outlets or equipment may be energised.</p>

      <h2>Content on this site</h2>
      <p>Articles here are general information about egress windows, building code, and property issues in Nebraska. They are not legal advice, engineering advice, or a substitute for a licensed contractor or engineer assessing your specific foundation. Code interpretation and enforcement vary between Lincoln-Lancaster County and the surrounding jurisdictions covering Waverly, Hickman, Ashland, Crete, Seward, and Beatrice — confirm anything consequential with the office that will issue your permit before acting on it.</p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>If anything here is unclear, or you want to know how a particular call was handled, <a href="/#contact">get in touch</a>.</p>

      <p style="margin-top:2.5rem;font-size:14px;color:#8A9AA5;">Last updated: 20 August 2026</p>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>
